Actually, wait. Let me reconsider `StringAppend`:

```c
static
void StringAppend(string* s, char c)
{
    if (s->len + 1 >= s->max)
    {
        char* new_ptr;
        s->max *= 10;
        new_ptr = AllocChunk(s->it8, s->max);
        memcpy(new_ptr, s->begin, s->len);
        s->begin = new_ptr;
    }
    s->begin[s->len++] = c;
    s->begin[s->len] = 0;
}
```

There's a subtle issue. `s->max` is `cmsInt32Number` (signed 32-bit). When `s->max *= 10` overflows, it becomes negative. Then `AllocChunk(s->it8, s->max)` is called with a negative `s->max` which gets converted to `cmsUInt32Number` (huge value). This would allocate a huge block. But this requires a string of length > 2^31/10.

